<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles Warren</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1840">1840</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Charles Warren was a British army officer and archaeologist. He surveyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in the 1860s, and in 1884–1885 led an expedition that ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics of Stellaland and Goshen and secured British Bechuanaland. As Commissioner of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Metropolitan Police from 1886 to 1888 he faced the Whitechapel murders attributed to Jack the Ripper.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-warren.jpg" alt="Charles Warren" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Herbert Rose Barraud of London · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Warren_carbon_print_portrait_by_Herbert_Rose_Barraud_of_London.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
